<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-22" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Time Dilation </title>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the time dilation result just derived,
        <m>
            ~{\Delta t_{g}=\gamma \Delta t_{s}}
        </m>, how are we supposed to tell <q>who is moving</q>? Doesn't the 
        principle of relativity tell us that being in motion and being at rest
        is equivalent? What distinguishes
        <m>
            \Delta t_{g}
        </m>
        and
        <m>
            \Delta t_{s}
        </m>
        ?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        Is there any situation when a proper time is equal to a coordinate time?
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        The following problem uses data from the film <q>Time dilation - an 
        experiment with mu mesons</q>. Muons, the electron's heavier cousins,
        are often created in the upper atmosphere, moving at speeds close to 
        the speed of light.
    </p>
    <p>
    In this experiment, muons of a specific energy (therefore, moving at a 
    specific speed with respect to Earth) were observed at the top of 
    Mt. Washington, roughly 2000m above sea level. Roughly 565 muons were 
    observed in the detector every hour.
    </p>
    <p> 
    Muons are unstable, and this 
    experiment also measured the muon half-life to be approximately 1.52
        <m>
            \mu\text{s}
        </m>
        . This means that if you have a bottle of muons, 1.52
        <m>
            \mu\text{s}
        </m>
        later, half of them will have decayed.
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                The muon lifetime as defined above is an intrinsic property of the
                particle.
                If that's the case, does it refer to a measurement in a particular
                reference frame?
                Which one?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Muons move <m>\textbf{very close}</m> to the speed of light.
                How long does it take for the muons at the top of Mt.
                Washington to reach sea level?
                How many half-lives have passed?
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Estimate the number of muons per hour that this experiment would
                observe when repeated at sea level (for this part, it's OK to round
                the number of half-lives passed to the nearest integer, as you'll
                see in just a moment).
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                When the experiment is repeated at sea level, you instead find 410
                muons per hour!
                Our expectation for the number of muons per hour should be, using
                the radioactive decay law,
                <m>N(t)=(565)\times\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{t/T}</m>, where
                <m>T=1.52\mu\text{s}</m>.
                Use this to find out how long the <q>trip</q> from the top of Mt.
                Washington to sea level takes in the muon frame.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                How fast were the muons moving?
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

  </exercise>
</worksheet>
